  "a3a-s21": {
    id: "a3a-s21", name: "Was the 1780s a critical period?", intro: "Use the passage below to answer the questions that follow.",
    sources: [{ title: "Source", blocks: [
      { p: "For generations, historians called the 1780s the \"critical period,\" when a feeble Confederation nearly let the new nation collapse until the Constitution rescued it. Other historians have pushed back. They point out that the Confederation Congress won the war, negotiated a favorable peace, and passed land ordinances that set up an orderly way to survey western lands and turn territories into equal states. The economic slump of the mid-1780s, they argue, was already easing by 1787. In this view, the sense of crisis came largely from creditors, merchants, and nationalists who wanted a stronger central government and used events such as Shays' Rebellion to win support for it." },
      { cite: PRACTICE, kind: "practice" },
    ] }],
  },

  // ---------- free response sources ----------
  "a3a-s22": {
    id: "a3a-s22", name: "How radical was the Revolution?", intro: "Use the passages below to answer all parts of the question that follows.",
    sources: [
      { title: "Source 1", blocks: [
        { p: "The American Revolution was far more radical than its orderly appearance suggests. Colonial society before 1760 rested on hierarchy: ordinary people deferred to gentlemen, and patronage and family ties decided who held power. The Revolution destroyed the legitimacy of that order. Once Americans declared that all men were created equal and that authority came from the people, ordinary farmers, artisans, and shopkeepers began to demand a voice. Within a generation, men who would once have bowed to their betters were running for office, organizing parties, and starting businesses. The Revolution did not free every American, but it made equality the central value of American life and set loose forces that no elite could control." },
        { cite: PRACTICE, kind: "practice" },
      ] },
      { title: "Source 2", blocks: [
        { p: "Measured by who gained and who did not, the American Revolution changed far less than its rhetoric promised. Wealthy merchants and planters led the movement and kept control of the new governments, which limited voting and officeholding to men of property. Most women gained no new rights, and married women still lost their legal identity to their husbands. Slavery survived and, in the South, grew stronger, protected by the new Constitution. Native nations, many of which had sided with Britain, lost land and leverage once the British left. For most Americans who were not white men of property, the Revolution replaced one set of rulers with another and left the structure of society largely in place." },
        { cite: PRACTICE, kind: "practice" },
      ] },
    ],
  },
